[{"input":"# Lecture 7 – Operating Systems\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain why operating systems exist.\n- Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.\n- Distinguish between applications and the operating system.\n- Identify major operating system families.\n- Understand the concepts of processes and threads.\n- Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.\n- Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.\n- Explain the purpose of device drivers.\n- Understand how file systems organize data.\n- Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapters, we learned about:\n- Computer hardware\n- Machine instructions\n- Programming\n- Software development\n\nA natural question now arises:\n\n> How do all these software applications share a single computer?\n\nConsider a typical laptop.\n\nAt the same time, it may be running:\n- A web browser\n- A music player\n- A word processor\n- Antivirus software\n- Several background services\n\nHow is this possible?\n\nThe answer is the **Operating System**.\n\nThe operating system acts as the manager of the computer.\n\n## What Is an Operating System?\n\nAn Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.\n\nExamples include:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Position in the Computing Stack\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nApplications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.\n\n### Main Responsibilities\n\nAn operating system manages:\n- Processors\n- Memory\n- Files\n- Storage\n- Network connections\n- Devices\n- Security\n\nWithout an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.\n\n## Life Without an Operating System\n\nTo appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.\n\nA programmer would need to:\n- Manage memory manually\n- Communicate directly with devices\n- Control storage hardware\n- Handle keyboard and screen operations\n- Coordinate multiple programs\n\nEvery application would repeat the same work.\n\n### Problems\n\nWithout an operating system:\n- Software would become extremely complex.\n- Applications could easily interfere with each other.\n- Security would be poor.\n- Hardware compatibility would be difficult.\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a large office building.\nWithout management:\n- Rooms are assigned randomly.\n- Resources are shared inefficiently.\n- Conflicts occur constantly.\n\nThe operating system acts as the building manager.","output":"","result":"# Lecture 7 – Operating Systems\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain why operating systems exist.\n- Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.\n- Distinguish between applications and the operating system.\n- Identify major operating system families.\n- Understand the concepts of processes and threads.\n- Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.\n- Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.\n- Explain the purpose of device drivers.\n- Understand how file systems organize data.\n- Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapters, we learned about:\n- Computer hardware\n- Machine instructions\n- Programming\n- Software development\n\nA natural question now arises:\n\n> How do all these software applications share a single computer?\n\nConsider a typical laptop.\n\nAt the same time, it may be running:\n- A web browser\n- A music player\n- A word processor\n- Antivirus software\n- Several background services\n\nHow is this possible?\n\nThe answer is the **Operating System**.\n\nThe operating system acts as the manager of the computer.\n\n## What Is an Operating System?\n\nAn Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.\n\nExamples include:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n- iOS\n\n### Position in the Computing Stack\n\n```text\nUser\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware\n```\n\nApplications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.\n\n### Main Responsibilities\n\nAn operating system manages:\n- Processors\n- Memory\n- Files\n- Storage\n- Network connections\n- Devices\n- Security\n\nWithout an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.\n\n## Life Without an Operating System\n\nTo appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.\n\nA programmer would need to:\n- Manage memory manually\n- Communicate directly with devices\n- Control storage hardware\n- Handle keyboard and screen operations\n- Coordinate multiple programs\n\nEvery application would repeat the same work.\n\n### Problems\n\nWithout an operating system:\n- Software would become extremely complex.\n- Applications could easily interfere with each other.\n- Security would be poor.\n- Hardware compatibility would be difficult.\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a large office building.\nWithout management:\n- Rooms are assigned randomly.\n- Resources are shared inefficiently.\n- Conflicts occur constantly.\n\nThe operating system acts as the building manager.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-lecture-7-operating-systems\">\n<h1>Lecture 7 – Operating Systems</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain why operating systems exist.</li>\n<li>Describe the main responsibilities of an operating system.</li>\n<li>Distinguish between applications and the operating system.</li>\n<li>Identify major operating system families.</li>\n<li>Understand the concepts of processes and threads.</li>\n<li>Explain virtual memory at a conceptual level.</li>\n<li>Understand the role of APIs, system calls, and libraries.</li>\n<li>Explain the purpose of device drivers.</li>\n<li>Understand how file systems organize data.</li>\n<li>Distinguish between user mode and kernel mode.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>In the previous chapters, we learned about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Computer hardware</li>\n<li>Machine instructions</li>\n<li>Programming</li>\n<li>Software development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A natural question now arises:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do all these software applications share a single computer?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Consider a typical laptop.</p>\n<p>At the same time, it may be running:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A web browser</li>\n<li>A music player</li>\n<li>A word processor</li>\n<li>Antivirus software</li>\n<li>Several background services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How is this possible?</p>\n<p>The answer is the <strong>Operating System</strong>.</p>\n<p>The operating system acts as the manager of the computer.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-what-is-an-operating-system\">\n<h1>What Is an Operating System?</h1>\n<p>An Operating System (OS) is software that manages computer resources and provides services to applications.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows</li>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>macOS</li>\n<li>Android</li>\n<li>iOS</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-position-in-the-computing-stack\">\n<h1>Position in the Computing Stack</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">User\n↓\nApplications\n↓\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware</pre>\n<p>Applications interact with the operating system, which in turn interacts with hardware.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-main-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Main Responsibilities</h1>\n<p>An operating system manages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Processors</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n<li>Files</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n<li>Network connections</li>\n<li>Devices</li>\n<li>Security</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without an operating system, every application would need to control hardware directly.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-life-without-an-operating-system\">\n<h1>Life Without an Operating System</h1>\n<p>To appreciate the role of an operating system, imagine a computer without one.</p>\n<p>A programmer would need to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manage memory manually</li>\n<li>Communicate directly with devices</li>\n<li>Control storage hardware</li>\n<li>Handle keyboard and screen operations</li>\n<li>Coordinate multiple programs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every application would repeat the same work.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-problems\">\n<h1>Problems</h1>\n<p>Without an operating system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Software would become extremely complex.</li>\n<li>Applications could easily interfere with each other.</li>\n<li>Security would be poor.</li>\n<li>Hardware compatibility would be difficult.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3421-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Think of a large office building.\nWithout management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rooms are assigned randomly.</li>\n<li>Resources are shared inefficiently.</li>\n<li>Conflicts occur constantly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The operating system acts as the building manager.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Operating System Families\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Widely used on personal computers and business environments.\n- Known for broad application support.\n\n### Linux\n- Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.\n- Also available for desktop systems.\n- Linux is open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Developed for Apple computers.\n- Known for tight integration between hardware and software.\n\n### Android\n- Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.\n- Based on Linux technologies.\n\n### iOS\n- Used on Apple mobile devices.\n- Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.\n\n## Resource Management\n\nOne of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.\n\nResources include:\n- CPU time\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Network access\n- Peripheral devices\n\n### Why Resource Management Is Needed\n\nSuppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.\n\nWho gets access?\n\nThe operating system decides.\n\n### Goal\n\nThe OS attempts to:\n- Share resources fairly\n- Maximize performance\n- Prevent conflicts\n\n## Processes\n\nA process is a running instance of a program.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose you open:\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nThe operating system creates a process for Chrome.\n\n### Multiple Processes\n\nYour computer may simultaneously run:\n- Browser process\n- Word processor process\n- Music player process\n- Background operating system processes\n\nEach process has:\n- Its own memory\n- Its own data\n- Its own execution state\n\n### Why Processes Matter\n\nProcesses provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.\n\n## Process Scheduling\n\nMost computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.\n\nTherefore the CPU is shared.\n\n### Scheduling\n\nThe operating system rapidly switches between processes.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nThis occurs extremely quickly.\n\n### User Experience\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nEven though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.\n\n### Analogy\n\nA teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.\n\n## Threads\n\nA process can contain one or more threads.\n\nA thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.\n\n### Example\n\nA web browser may:\n- Display a webpage\n- Download images\n- Play audio\n\nsimultaneously.\n\nThese tasks may be handled by different threads.\n\n### Benefits\n\nThreads improve:\n- Responsiveness\n- Performance\n- Resource utilization\n\n### Analogy\n\nA company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.","output":"","result":"## Operating System Families\n\n### Microsoft Windows\n- Widely used on personal computers and business environments.\n- Known for broad application support.\n\n### Linux\n- Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.\n- Also available for desktop systems.\n- Linux is open source.\n\n### macOS\n- Developed for Apple computers.\n- Known for tight integration between hardware and software.\n\n### Android\n- Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.\n- Based on Linux technologies.\n\n### iOS\n- Used on Apple mobile devices.\n- Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.\n\n## Resource Management\n\nOne of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.\n\nResources include:\n- CPU time\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Network access\n- Peripheral devices\n\n### Why Resource Management Is Needed\n\nSuppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.\n\nWho gets access?\n\nThe operating system decides.\n\n### Goal\n\nThe OS attempts to:\n- Share resources fairly\n- Maximize performance\n- Prevent conflicts\n\n## Processes\n\nA process is a running instance of a program.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose you open:\n\n```text\nGoogle Chrome\n```\n\nThe operating system creates a process for Chrome.\n\n### Multiple Processes\n\nYour computer may simultaneously run:\n- Browser process\n- Word processor process\n- Music player process\n- Background operating system processes\n\nEach process has:\n- Its own memory\n- Its own data\n- Its own execution state\n\n### Why Processes Matter\n\nProcesses provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.\n\n## Process Scheduling\n\nMost computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.\n\nTherefore the CPU is shared.\n\n### Scheduling\n\nThe operating system rapidly switches between processes.\n\n```text\nProcess A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...\n```\n\nThis occurs extremely quickly.\n\n### User Experience\n\n```text\nEverything running simultaneously.\n```\n\nEven though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.\n\n### Analogy\n\nA teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.\n\n## Threads\n\nA process can contain one or more threads.\n\nA thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.\n\n### Example\n\nA web browser may:\n- Display a webpage\n- Download images\n- Play audio\n\nsimultaneously.\n\nThese tasks may be handled by different threads.\n\n### Benefits\n\nThreads improve:\n- Responsiveness\n- Performance\n- Resource utilization\n\n### Analogy\n\nA company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-operating-system-families\">\n<h1>Operating System Families</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-microsoft-windows\">\n<h1>Microsoft Windows</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Widely used on personal computers and business environments.</li>\n<li>Known for broad application support.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-linux\">\n<h1>Linux</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Commonly used on servers, supercomputers, and embedded systems.</li>\n<li>Also available for desktop systems.</li>\n<li>Linux is open source.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-macos\">\n<h1>macOS</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed for Apple computers.</li>\n<li>Known for tight integration between hardware and software.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-android\">\n<h1>Android</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Primarily used on smartphones and tablets.</li>\n<li>Based on Linux technologies.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-ios\">\n<h1>iOS</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Used on Apple mobile devices.</li>\n<li>Designed specifically for smartphones and tablets.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-resource-management\">\n<h1>Resource Management</h1>\n<p>One of the most important responsibilities of an operating system is resource management.</p>\n<p>Resources include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU time</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n<li>Network access</li>\n<li>Peripheral devices</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-why-resource-management-is-needed\">\n<h1>Why Resource Management Is Needed</h1>\n<p>Suppose three applications simultaneously want to use the CPU.</p>\n<p>Who gets access?</p>\n<p>The operating system decides.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-goal\">\n<h1>Goal</h1>\n<p>The OS attempts to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Share resources fairly</li>\n<li>Maximize performance</li>\n<li>Prevent conflicts</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-processes\">\n<h1>Processes</h1>\n<p>A process is a running instance of a program.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Suppose you open:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Google Chrome</pre>\n<p>The operating system creates a process for Chrome.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-multiple-processes\">\n<h1>Multiple Processes</h1>\n<p>Your computer may simultaneously run:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Browser process</li>\n<li>Word processor process</li>\n<li>Music player process</li>\n<li>Background operating system processes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each process has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its own memory</li>\n<li>Its own data</li>\n<li>Its own execution state</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-why-processes-matter\">\n<h1>Why Processes Matter</h1>\n<p>Processes provide isolation.\nA crash in one process usually does not crash the entire system.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-process-scheduling\">\n<h1>Process Scheduling</h1>\n<p>Most computers have fewer CPU cores than active processes.</p>\n<p>Therefore the CPU is shared.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-scheduling\">\n<h1>Scheduling</h1>\n<p>The operating system rapidly switches between processes.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Process A\nProcess B\nProcess C\nProcess A\nProcess B\n...</pre>\n<p>This occurs extremely quickly.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-user-experience\">\n<h1>User Experience</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Everything running simultaneously.</pre>\n<p>Even though the processor is constantly switching between tasks.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>A teacher answering questions from many students.\nThe teacher switches attention rapidly between them.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-threads\">\n<h1>Threads</h1>\n<p>A process can contain one or more threads.</p>\n<p>A thread represents a sequence of execution within a process.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>A web browser may:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Display a webpage</li>\n<li>Download images</li>\n<li>Play audio</li>\n</ul>\n<p>simultaneously.</p>\n<p>These tasks may be handled by different threads.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Threads improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Responsiveness</li>\n<li>Performance</li>\n<li>Resource utilization</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3423-analogy-2\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>A company is a process.\nEmployees working on different tasks are threads.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Memory Management\n\nPrograms need memory to store:\n- Variables\n- Documents\n- Images\n- Running code\n\nThe operating system manages this memory.\n\n### Challenges\n\nThe OS must ensure:\n- Programs receive memory.\n- Programs do not overwrite each other's memory.\n- Memory is efficiently utilized.\n\n### Protection\n\nApplications should not access memory belonging to other applications.\n\nThis prevents:\n- Crashes\n- Data corruption\n- Security problems\n\n## Virtual Memory\n\nModern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.\n\nVirtual memory helps solve this problem.\n\n### Basic Idea\n\nApplications believe they have access to a very large memory space.\n\nThe operating system maps this virtual memory onto:\n- RAM\n- Storage\n\nas needed.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nThe operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.\n\n### Benefits\n\nVirtual memory provides:\n- Flexibility\n- Isolation\n- Simplified programming","output":"","result":"## Memory Management\n\nPrograms need memory to store:\n- Variables\n- Documents\n- Images\n- Running code\n\nThe operating system manages this memory.\n\n### Challenges\n\nThe OS must ensure:\n- Programs receive memory.\n- Programs do not overwrite each other's memory.\n- Memory is efficiently utilized.\n\n### Protection\n\nApplications should not access memory belonging to other applications.\n\nThis prevents:\n- Crashes\n- Data corruption\n- Security problems\n\n## Virtual Memory\n\nModern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.\n\nVirtual memory helps solve this problem.\n\n### Basic Idea\n\nApplications believe they have access to a very large memory space.\n\nThe operating system maps this virtual memory onto:\n- RAM\n- Storage\n\nas needed.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nRAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB\n```\n\nThe operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.\n\n### Benefits\n\nVirtual memory provides:\n- Flexibility\n- Isolation\n- Simplified programming","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-memory-management\">\n<h1>Memory Management</h1>\n<p>Programs need memory to store:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Variables</li>\n<li>Documents</li>\n<li>Images</li>\n<li>Running code</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The operating system manages this memory.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-challenges\">\n<h1>Challenges</h1>\n<p>The OS must ensure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Programs receive memory.</li>\n<li>Programs do not overwrite each other’s memory.</li>\n<li>Memory is efficiently utilized.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-protection\">\n<h1>Protection</h1>\n<p>Applications should not access memory belonging to other applications.</p>\n<p>This prevents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Crashes</li>\n<li>Data corruption</li>\n<li>Security problems</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-virtual-memory\">\n<h1>Virtual Memory</h1>\n<p>Modern computers often run programs requiring more memory than is physically available.</p>\n<p>Virtual memory helps solve this problem.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-basic-idea\">\n<h1>Basic Idea</h1>\n<p>Applications believe they have access to a very large memory space.</p>\n<p>The operating system maps this virtual memory onto:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RAM</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>as needed.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">RAM = 16 GB\nProgram requires = 20 GB</pre>\n<p>The operating system can temporarily store less-used information on storage.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3424-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Virtual memory provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexibility</li>\n<li>Isolation</li>\n<li>Simplified programming</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## User Mode and Kernel Mode\n\nModern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.\n\n### User Mode\n\nMost applications run in:\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nRestrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Control hardware\n- Access protected memory\n- Modify operating system internals\n\n### Kernel Mode\n\nThe operating system's core runs in:\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nThis mode has full access to the system.\n\n### Why Separation Matters\n\nOne software bug could:\n- Corrupt data\n- Damage system stability\n- Create major security risks\n\nMode separation helps prevent such problems.\n\n## Libraries\n\nApplications often require common functionality.\n\nExamples:\n- Displaying windows\n- Connecting to networks\n- Reading files\n\nRather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\nLibraries improve:\n- Productivity\n- Reliability\n- Reusability\n\n## System Calls\n\nApplications occasionally need operating system services.\n\nThey request these services through system calls.\n\n### Examples\n\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Read a file from storage\n- Allocate memory\n- Access hardware\n\nInstead they ask the operating system.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)\n\nAn API is a defined way for software components to communicate.\n\n### Purpose\n\nAPIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Simplicity\n- Portability\n- Modularity\n\n## Device Drivers\n\nComputers interact with many hardware devices.\n\nExamples:\n- Printers\n- Keyboards\n- Screens\n- Cameras\n- Network cards\n\nA device driver serves as a translator between:\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## File Systems\n\nData must be organized efficiently.\n\nThe file system provides this organization.\n\n### Responsibilities\n\n- Files\n- Directories (folders)\n- File names\n- Storage locations\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services and Background Processes\n\nNot all software is visible to users.\n\nExamples:\n- Security software\n- Networking services\n- Cloud synchronization\n- Software update services\n\nThese services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.","output":"","result":"## User Mode and Kernel Mode\n\nModern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.\n\n### User Mode\n\nMost applications run in:\n\n```text\nUser Mode\n```\n\nRestrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Control hardware\n- Access protected memory\n- Modify operating system internals\n\n### Kernel Mode\n\nThe operating system's core runs in:\n\n```text\nKernel Mode\n```\n\nThis mode has full access to the system.\n\n### Why Separation Matters\n\nOne software bug could:\n- Corrupt data\n- Damage system stability\n- Create major security risks\n\nMode separation helps prevent such problems.\n\n## Libraries\n\nApplications often require common functionality.\n\nExamples:\n- Displaying windows\n- Connecting to networks\n- Reading files\n\nRather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nOpen File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\nLibraries improve:\n- Productivity\n- Reliability\n- Reusability\n\n## System Calls\n\nApplications occasionally need operating system services.\n\nThey request these services through system calls.\n\n### Examples\n\nApplications cannot directly:\n- Read a file from storage\n- Allocate memory\n- Access hardware\n\nInstead they ask the operating system.\n\n```text\nApplication\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware\n```\n\n## Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)\n\nAn API is a defined way for software components to communicate.\n\n### Purpose\n\nAPIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Simplicity\n- Portability\n- Modularity\n\n## Device Drivers\n\nComputers interact with many hardware devices.\n\nExamples:\n- Printers\n- Keyboards\n- Screens\n- Cameras\n- Network cards\n\nA device driver serves as a translator between:\n\n```text\nOperating System\n↓\nHardware Device\n```\n\n## File Systems\n\nData must be organized efficiently.\n\nThe file system provides this organization.\n\n### Responsibilities\n\n- Files\n- Directories (folders)\n- File names\n- Storage locations\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nDocuments\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf\n```\n\n## Services and Background Processes\n\nNot all software is visible to users.\n\nExamples:\n- Security software\n- Networking services\n- Cloud synchronization\n- Software update services\n\nThese services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-user-mode-and-kernel-mode\">\n<h1>User Mode and Kernel Mode</h1>\n<p>Modern operating systems separate software into two execution modes.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-user-mode\">\n<h1>User Mode</h1>\n<p>Most applications run in:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">User Mode</pre>\n<p>Restrictions apply.\nApplications cannot directly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control hardware</li>\n<li>Access protected memory</li>\n<li>Modify operating system internals</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-kernel-mode\">\n<h1>Kernel Mode</h1>\n<p>The operating system’s core runs in:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Kernel Mode</pre>\n<p>This mode has full access to the system.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-why-separation-matters\">\n<h1>Why Separation Matters</h1>\n<p>One software bug could:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corrupt data</li>\n<li>Damage system stability</li>\n<li>Create major security risks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mode separation helps prevent such problems.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-libraries\">\n<h1>Libraries</h1>\n<p>Applications often require common functionality.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Displaying windows</li>\n<li>Connecting to networks</li>\n<li>Reading files</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rather than rewriting this functionality repeatedly, developers use libraries.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Open File\nPrint Document\nConnect to Network</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-benefits\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<p>Libraries improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Productivity</li>\n<li>Reliability</li>\n<li>Reusability</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-system-calls\">\n<h1>System Calls</h1>\n<p>Applications occasionally need operating system services.</p>\n<p>They request these services through system calls.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-examples\">\n<h1>Examples</h1>\n<p>Applications cannot directly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Read a file from storage</li>\n<li>Allocate memory</li>\n<li>Access hardware</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Instead they ask the operating system.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Application\n    ↓\nSystem Call\n    ↓\nOperating System\n    ↓\nHardware</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-application-programming-interfaces-apis\">\n<h1>Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)</h1>\n<p>An API is a defined way for software components to communicate.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-purpose\">\n<h1>Purpose</h1>\n<p>APIs allow applications to use services without knowing internal implementation details.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-benefits-2\">\n<h1>Benefits</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Simplicity</li>\n<li>Portability</li>\n<li>Modularity</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-device-drivers\">\n<h1>Device Drivers</h1>\n<p>Computers interact with many hardware devices.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Printers</li>\n<li>Keyboards</li>\n<li>Screens</li>\n<li>Cameras</li>\n<li>Network cards</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A device driver serves as a translator between:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Operating System\n↓\nHardware Device</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-file-systems\">\n<h1>File Systems</h1>\n<p>Data must be organized efficiently.</p>\n<p>The file system provides this organization.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-responsibilities\">\n<h1>Responsibilities</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Files</li>\n<li>Directories (folders)</li>\n<li>File names</li>\n<li>Storage locations</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Documents\n├─ Essay.docx\n├─ Budget.xlsx\n└─ Notes.pdf</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3425-services-and-background-processes\">\n<h1>Services and Background Processes</h1>\n<p>Not all software is visible to users.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Security software</li>\n<li>Networking services</li>\n<li>Cloud synchronization</li>\n<li>Software update services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These services perform supporting tasks while users work with applications.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Summary\n\n- The operating system manages computer resources.\n- The OS sits between applications and hardware.\n- Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.\n- Processes represent running programs.\n- Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.\n- Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.\n- Memory management allocates and protects memory.\n- Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.\n- User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.\n- Libraries provide reusable functionality.\n- System calls allow applications to request operating system services.\n- APIs define interfaces between software components.\n- Device drivers enable communication with hardware.\n- File systems organize and manage stored data.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Operating System (OS) | Software that manages hardware and provides services |\n| Process | Running instance of a program |\n| Thread | Execution path within a process |\n| Scheduling | Allocation of CPU time among processes |\n| Virtual Memory | Abstraction that extends usable memory |\n| User Mode | Restricted execution mode for applications |\n| Kernel Mode | Privileged execution mode for the operating system |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software functions |\n| System Call | Request from an application to the operating system |\n| API | Interface enabling software communication |\n| Device Driver | Software translating between OS and hardware |\n| File System | Organization of files and folders on storage |\n| Service | Background software providing system functionality |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is the purpose of an operating system?\n- Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?\n- Name five major operating system families.\n- What is a process?\n- What is the difference between a process and a thread?\n- Why is process scheduling necessary?\n- What is virtual memory?\n- Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?\n- What is the purpose of a system call?\n- What is an API?\n- Why are device drivers necessary?\n- What does a file system do?\n- Explain what happens when a document is opened.\n- Why are background services important?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 8\n\nSo far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study **computer networks and the Internet**, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.","output":"","result":"## Summary\n\n- The operating system manages computer resources.\n- The OS sits between applications and hardware.\n- Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.\n- Processes represent running programs.\n- Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.\n- Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.\n- Memory management allocates and protects memory.\n- Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.\n- User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.\n- Libraries provide reusable functionality.\n- System calls allow applications to request operating system services.\n- APIs define interfaces between software components.\n- Device drivers enable communication with hardware.\n- File systems organize and manage stored data.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Operating System (OS) | Software that manages hardware and provides services |\n| Process | Running instance of a program |\n| Thread | Execution path within a process |\n| Scheduling | Allocation of CPU time among processes |\n| Virtual Memory | Abstraction that extends usable memory |\n| User Mode | Restricted execution mode for applications |\n| Kernel Mode | Privileged execution mode for the operating system |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software functions |\n| System Call | Request from an application to the operating system |\n| API | Interface enabling software communication |\n| Device Driver | Software translating between OS and hardware |\n| File System | Organization of files and folders on storage |\n| Service | Background software providing system functionality |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is the purpose of an operating system?\n- Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?\n- Name five major operating system families.\n- What is a process?\n- What is the difference between a process and a thread?\n- Why is process scheduling necessary?\n- What is virtual memory?\n- Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?\n- What is the purpose of a system call?\n- What is an API?\n- Why are device drivers necessary?\n- What does a file system do?\n- Explain what happens when a document is opened.\n- Why are background services important?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 8\n\nSo far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study **computer networks and the Internet**, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>The operating system manages computer resources.</li>\n<li>The OS sits between applications and hardware.</li>\n<li>Major operating system families include Windows, Linux, macOS, Android, and iOS.</li>\n<li>Processes represent running programs.</li>\n<li>Threads provide multiple execution paths within a process.</li>\n<li>Scheduling enables many programs to share the CPU.</li>\n<li>Memory management allocates and protects memory.</li>\n<li>Virtual memory gives programs the illusion of large memory spaces.</li>\n<li>User mode and kernel mode improve stability and security.</li>\n<li>Libraries provide reusable functionality.</li>\n<li>System calls allow applications to request operating system services.</li>\n<li>APIs define interfaces between software components.</li>\n<li>Device drivers enable communication with hardware.</li>\n<li>File systems organize and manage stored data.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating System (OS)</td>\n<td>Software that manages hardware and provides services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Process</td>\n<td>Running instance of a program</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thread</td>\n<td>Execution path within a process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scheduling</td>\n<td>Allocation of CPU time among processes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Virtual Memory</td>\n<td>Abstraction that extends usable memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User Mode</td>\n<td>Restricted execution mode for applications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kernel Mode</td>\n<td>Privileged execution mode for the operating system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Library</td>\n<td>Reusable collection of software functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Call</td>\n<td>Request from an application to the operating system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API</td>\n<td>Interface enabling software communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Device Driver</td>\n<td>Software translating between OS and hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File System</td>\n<td>Organization of files and folders on storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service</td>\n<td>Background software providing system functionality</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the purpose of an operating system?</li>\n<li>Why would programming be difficult without an operating system?</li>\n<li>Name five major operating system families.</li>\n<li>What is a process?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between a process and a thread?</li>\n<li>Why is process scheduling necessary?</li>\n<li>What is virtual memory?</li>\n<li>Why are user mode and kernel mode separated?</li>\n<li>What is the purpose of a system call?</li>\n<li>What is an API?</li>\n<li>Why are device drivers necessary?</li>\n<li>What does a file system do?</li>\n<li>Explain what happens when a document is opened.</li>\n<li>Why are background services important?</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture07-c3426-transition-to-lecture-8\">\n<h1>Transition to Lecture 8</h1>\n<p>So far, we have explored how a single computer works and how its operating system manages resources. However, modern computing rarely happens in isolation.</p>\n<p>In the next lecture, we will study <strong>computer networks and the Internet</strong>, and learn how computers communicate, exchange information, and provide services across the world.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]